<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sino-French War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Tonkin War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sino-French War was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> from August 1884 to April 1885 over control of Tonkin in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. France destroyed the Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> and fought in Tonkin and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, but a reverse at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a> in March 1885 brought down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry">Jules Ferry</a>'s government. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> of June 1885 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s claims and confirmed the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Vietnam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-french-war.jpg" alt="Sino-French War" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinoFrenchWar1884-1885.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>French interest in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> dated from the late 18th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> began its colonial campaign in 1858 and formed the colony of Cochinchina in 1862. French explorers hoped the Red River would give a trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flag Army</a>, a private army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a>, taxed its trade heavily. In 1873 Lieutenant Francis Garnier attacked northern Vietnam beyond his instructions and was killed by the Black Flags at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, and in 1882 Commandant Henri Rivière stormed the citadel of Hanoi against his orders.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, long a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, asked for help. China armed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-flag-army">Black Flags</a> and covertly opposed the French, and in 1882 Chinese regular armies occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>, Bắc Ninh and other towns. On 20 August 1883 Admiral Amédée Courbet stormed the forts at Thuận An and forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hue-1884">Treaty of Huế</a>, placing Tonkin under French protection. Talks between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and China in 1883 failed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Negotiations broke down in mid-August 1884. On 23 August Courbet's squadron destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s Fujian fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> in a two-hour engagement, sinking nine Chinese ships in less than an hour. Chinese losses may have amounted to 3,000 dead, while French losses were minimal. Neither country declared war. The defeat brought patriotic fervour in China, marred by attacks on foreigners.</p><p>In Tonkin, General Brière de l'Isle beat off a Chinese invasion of the Red River Delta in the Kép Campaign of October 1884. French troops shot or bayoneted scores of wounded Chinese soldiers after Kép, and prisoners were rarely taken by either side. On 23 February 1885 General de Négrier won the Battle of Đồng Đăng. The French later withdrew in haste from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>, which Qing forces held by early April. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> they occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a> but advanced no further in seven months. Fighting ended on 4 April 1885.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The peace treaty of June 1885 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> control of Annam and Tonkin. The French evacuated Formosa and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a> and dropped demands for an indemnity. In 1887 Cochinchina, Annam, Tonkin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> were joined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> followed after 1893. French pacification of Tonkin lasted about a decade, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-yongfu">Liu Yongfu</a>'s forces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/can-vuong">Cần Vương</a> insurgents still fighting.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the war ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jules-ferry">Jules Ferry</a>'s career, and his successor Henri Brisson resigned after the Tonkin Debate of December 1885, in which Clemenceau and other opponents of expansion nearly won a withdrawal. The Chamber voted the 1886 credits by 274 votes to 270. Some Chinese commanders protested the end of the fighting, and the war strengthened the dominance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> over the Qing government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sino-French War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the deaths of Francis Garnier in 1873 and Henri Rivière in 1883 in Tonkin, the Harmand and Patenôtre treaties of 1883–1884 made central and northern Vietnam the protectorates of Annam and Tonkin under the Nguyen emperors, and the <b>Sino-French War</b> of 1884–1885 forced China to renounce its suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its colonial armies fought the <b>Sino-French War</b> of 1884–1885, in which Admiral Courbet destroyed the Chinese fleet at Fuzhou, and suppressed rebellions in Morocco in 1925, Syria in 1926 and Indochina in 1930.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1873 and 1882–1883 French forces seized the Hanoi citadel, and Chinese troops and the Black Flag army of Liu Yongfu fought them in Tonkin until the <b>Sino-French War</b> of 1884–1885 ended Qing involvement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Sino-French War</b> a French squadron destroyed the Fujian fleet and bombarded the navy yard at Fuzhou in August 1884, although Qing armies fought well on land.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Sino-French War</b> of 1884 to 1885 ended Qing claims to the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tonkin">French protectorate of Tonkin</a></li></ul></section>
